 provide  immunity  for how long all of those things  so there's a lot of moving parts to the  some  difficulty that we're in and the  use of travel bubbles or travel circuits  is one way that we're getting  uh tourism travel accommodations and all  of that  going again so europe will definitely be  one of the um  cases of seeing how things go and how  how we can get things  um successful again yeah you mentioned  about  how some people are just desperate to  get out there and travel again  is there a flip side to that and that  there are maybe now people who have  taken a liking to traveling within the  countries in which they already live  and that international tourism may not  get back to the position that it was  before  well it would seem that we're in a  era of really profound change and i  don't think the travel we knew before  will come back the way it was before we  took uh  cheap travel for granted and we thought  that we could go anywhere we wanted you  know just on  whims and for very inexpensive very easy  sort of holidays  and it's likely that travel will be more  expensive  for the next few years it's probably not  going to be as convenient because there  won't be so many routes to travel  or so many uh ease of scheduling um to  happen  and we have to be ready for instance for  things like  uh quarantine measures to be put in  place uh for perhaps another year or so  so those difficulties are likely to  remain we will see i think  the term i would use is a localization  of travel a narrowing of travel circuits  and even people wanting to know their  home countries you know sometimes we  take  our home country for granted and we want  to travel to other places  uh for the difference for the exoticism  and so on  so i think that we will find this more  narrow circuits  all right freya higgins debiol from  university of  south australia thanks for joining us on  the kobe 19 special  thank you so much  and now it's the part of the program  where we put o